// Resources: an owned identity — a payload, a cleanup that runs exactly once, and a baton
// saying which fiber may use it.
//
// A resource is the one value that does not copy across fibers: there is only one file
// descriptor. Crossing a fiber boundary shares the IDENTITY (the runtime mints a fresh
// per-holder ref to one shared body); the BATON — the right to use it, and the duty to
// clean it up — moves only at the three transfer edges, all visible in source:
//
//     fiber::spawn(move () => handler(c));   // a `move` lambda
//     try channel::send(ch, c);              // a channel send (throw means NOT sent)
//     task::spawn(() => open(..))            // a task returning it to its awaiter
//
// After giving a resource away, the old bindings are stale names: using one throws
// NotOwnerError — a catchable error naming the mistake, where other languages hand you
// two writers interleaving on one socket. Cleanup runs when the owning ref dies — last
// use, scope end, explicit `release`, or the owning fiber's teardown (crash included) —
// so it is deterministic and exactly-once, with no atomics anywhere on the hot path.
//
// Two paths still must not collide, exactly as before the baton existed. Explicit
// release, where the caller wants to see the failure:
//
//     try resource::release(r)
//
// and automatic cleanup at the owning ref's death, where a drop has no error channel and
// the failure is discarded. Disarm-then-act is what keeps them exactly-once together.
//
// `E` rides on the cleanup's arrow (`(T) throws E -> ()`) rather than coming back as a
// union, which buys three things: `release` composes with `try` like anything else,
// infallible cleanup is `Resource[T, never]` whose `release` needs no `try` at all, and
// double release needs no sentinel — it disarms first, finds nothing to do, and returns.
//
// The body lives on the shared heap so it can outlive its creator: the payload is copied
// there at CONSTRUCTION (so a payload must be sendable when the resource is born), and a
// capturing cleanup's environment is copied there too — an unsendable capture fails at
// `new`, the one line where the resource's author is present to fix it.

use std::string;

/// The runtime object: a per-holder REF to a shared body. `@runtime` is what tells the
/// backend to represent this as a pointer to that C type instead of laying out a struct
/// from fields it does not have.
@runtime("neon_resource") sealed opaque record Resource[T, E] {}

/// Reading a resource whose cleanup has already run. Neither Rust (which prevents it with
/// borrowck) nor Swift (which does not prevent it) reports it; it costs one flag check on
/// operations that were about to make a syscall anyway.
record ReleasedError { message: str }
impl Error for ReleasedError { fn message(v: ReleasedError) -> str { v.message } }

/// Touching a resource whose baton this fiber does not hold: it was moved to another
/// fiber (or is mid-flight in a channel), or it arrived here as a name without the baton.
/// The failure the baton exists to make loud — the silent alternative is two fibers
/// interleaving writes on one socket.
record NotOwnerError { message: str }
impl Error for NotOwnerError { fn message(v: NotOwnerError) -> str { v.message } }

internal mod raw {
    /// Codegen-assisted: the payload crosses by address with its value-witness, and the
    /// drop is emitted per instantiation, since only that code knows how to call `cleanup`.
    @native("neon_resource_new")
    fn new[T, E](payload: T, cleanup: (T) throws E -> ()) -> Resource[T, E]

    /// Both use the native out-parameter convention: the tuple's tail is a C
    /// out-parameter, and the head is the gate's code — 0 ok (tail written), 1 released,
    /// 2 not-owner. The codes are `NEON_RESOURCE_*` in runtime/include/neon/resource.h.
    @native("neon_resource_get") fn get[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> (i64, T)
    @native("neon_resource_disarm") fn disarm[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> (i64, T)

    @native("neon_resource_is_live") fn is_live[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> bool
    @native("neon_resource_cleanup") fn cleanup[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> (T) throws E -> ()
}

fn new[T, E](payload: T, cleanup: (T) throws E -> ()) -> Resource[T, E] {
    raw::new(payload, cleanup)
}

/// The payload, or a diagnosable error: released if cleanup already ran, not-owner if the
/// baton lives on another fiber. This is what turns use-after-close and use-after-move
/// into something a program can report rather than a read of a stale descriptor.
fn get[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) throws ReleasedError | NotOwnerError -> T {
    let (code, payload) = raw::get(r);
    if code == 1 {
        throw ReleasedError { message: "this resource has already been released" };
    };
    if code == 2 {
        throw NotOwnerError { message: "this resource was moved to another fiber" };
    };
    payload
}

/// Run cleanup now and let its failure reach the caller. Idempotent, and not by holding
/// a sentinel: disarming first means a second call finds nothing to run and returns.
///
/// A NON-OWNER's release is the same nothing: a moved-away handle owes no cleanup from
/// here, so there is nothing to run — the double-release precedent, extended. This is a
/// deliberate asymmetry with `get`: reading a moved handle is a bug worth naming
/// (NotOwnerError), releasing one is a duty already discharged elsewhere. It is also what
/// keeps `release` throwing only `E`, so `Resource[T, never]`'s release composes without
/// `try` — the property the whole error design bought.
fn release[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) throws E {
    let (code, payload) = raw::disarm(r);
    if code == 0 {
        try raw::cleanup(r)(payload);
    };
}

/// Take the payload and disarm, without running cleanup. `null` if it was already gone —
/// released, or moved to another fiber (a non-owner takes nothing, silently, by the same
/// rule as `release`). The caller owns whatever came out.
fn take[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> T | null {
    let (code, payload) = raw::disarm(r);
    if code == 0 { payload } else { null }
}

fn is_live[T, E](r: Resource[T, E]) -> bool {
    raw::is_live(r)
}

/// Hold a resource for exactly the region of `body`, then release it.
///
/// Under last-use ARC a resource dies at its last *use*, which for a lock guard is the
/// `lock` call itself — so it would be released before the section it exists to protect.
/// The fix needs no language feature, only a function that touches the resource on the far
/// side of the body: because `r` is used after `body(...)` returns, the refcount pass must
/// keep it alive across the whole call. That is the entire trick.
///
/// The failure path is right for free. If `body` throws, `release` never runs explicitly,
/// but ARC releases `r` on the throwing edge and cleanup fires anyway: the normal path
/// observes the cleanup's error, the throwing path still cleans up silently.
fn using[T, E, R](r: Resource[T, E], body: (T) -> R) throws E | ReleasedError | NotOwnerError -> R {
    // `get` supplies the loud NotOwner path; `release` below is owner-silent by design.
    let payload = try get(r);
    let out = body(payload);
    // The last use of `r` is here, not at the `get` above.
    try release(r);
    out
}
